Notes on the translation:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- No namespace macros exist in [38;5;10minclude/lib.h[0m, so linker symbols are the plain source names. I exported all seven functions with external linkage in [38;5;10mlib.c[0m ([38;5;10mcomplexmode[0m, [38;5;10mcreate_result_string[0m, [38;5;10mcheck_permissions[0m, [38;5;10msafe_add[0m, [38;5;10mmultiply_with_log[0m, [38;5;10mcopy_and_sum[0m, [38;5;10mcompare_operations[0m) to mirror the shared library's symbol table.[0m[0m
- I call libc [38;5;10mprintf[0m/[38;5;10msnprintf[0m/[38;5;10mmalloc[0m/[38;5;10mfree[0m/[38;5;10mmemcpy[0m/[38;5;10mstrcmp[0m directly rather than using Rust's [38;5;10mstd::io[0m. This matters for byte-identity: C stdout is fully buffered when piped, and mixing Rust's [38;5;10mLineWriter[0m with a C caller's [38;5;10mprintf[0m would reorder output. It also keeps [38;5;10mcreate_result_string[0m's return pointer [38;5;10mfree()[0m-able by C callers, and makes [38;5;10mcompare_operations[0m return glibc's exact [38;5;10mstrcmp[0m values.[0m[0m
- Integer overflow uses [38;5;10mwrapping_*[0m so the C behavior (wraparound in practice) is reproduced instead of panicking in debug builds.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mcopy_and_sum[0m's size computation reproduces C's [38;5;10mcount * sizeof(int)[0m promotion to [38;5;10msize_t[0m, so a negative [38;5;10mcount[0m still becomes a huge failing allocation and prints "Memory allocation failed".[0m[0m
- Existing C quirks left intact: [38;5;10mpermissions[0m is [38;5;10m0644[0m, so mode 4 always takes the addition branch ([38;5;10m0644 & 0100 == 0[0m), and mode 2's empty-string check is unreachable. [38;5;10mEXEC_PERM[0m remains unused.[0m[0m
